{#
	**The placeholder, and what it is a placeholder for** (XIV-102).

	There is no payment gateway in this system. That is a decision with compliance
	weight behind it and it is deliberately not this ticket's, which exists so that
	pricing can land without waiting for one. So this page has to answer a question
	somebody is standing in front of — *how do I get this module* — without either
	pretending to take money or turning a self-service product into a phone call.

	The answer is [XIV-64]'s, one layer down: **record what they want, do nothing
	privileged, and let a person act on it.** That ticket separated "anyone may
	ask" from "the thing happens" for a public signup form; the same separation is
	right here, and reusing it means that the day a real gateway arrives it slots
	in where the operator currently stands rather than replacing a flow built
	around pretending.

	## What this page is not, item by item

	Every one of these is an absence somebody could mistake for work not yet done,
	so each is written down as a decision:

	  * **No card fields.** None, of any kind, disabled or otherwise. A form that
	    looks like checkout and quietly does nothing is worse than a sentence
	    saying what is actually going on, because it teaches people to type card
	    numbers into pages that do not take them — a habit worth not creating,
	    especially in software somebody uses at work every day.
	  * **No total, no line items, no VAT row.** The price is shown once, as what
	    the module costs, and nothing on this page adds anything up. A total is the
	    visual grammar of a page that is about to charge you.
	  * **No "processing", no spinner, no confirmation number.** The button posts a
	    row and redirects. There is no transaction to have a state.
	  * **No promise of when.** "Somebody will be in touch" is what is actually
	    true; an installation that said "within 24 hours" would be making a
	    commitment on behalf of a company this code knows nothing about.

	## And what it does say

	What it costs, once. That pressing the button is a *request* and not a payment.
	That nothing is charged now — said in as many words, because it is the sentence
	that stops somebody worrying about it afterwards. And that a person will get
	back to them, which is the honest description of what happens next.
#}
{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ 'store.buy_title'|trans({'%module%': offer.label}) }}{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
	{{ include('_topbar.html.twig') }}

	<main class="container page-shell">
		{{ include('_flashes.html.twig') }}

		<div class="d-flex align-items-center justify-content-between mb-3">
			<h1 class="h3 mb-0 d-flex align-items-center gap-2">
				{% if offer.blueprint.icon %}
					<i class="bi bi-{{ offer.blueprint.icon }} text-body-secondary" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				{% endif %}
				{{ 'store.buy_title'|trans({'%module%': offer.label}) }}
			</h1>
			<a class="btn btn-link" href="{{ path('store_module', {module: offer.key}) }}">{{ 'common.back'|trans }}</a>
		</div>

		<div class="row g-4">
			<div class="col-lg-7">
				<div class="card">
					<div class="card-body">
						{#
							The figure, large, once, and with nothing beneath it that looks
							like arithmetic. `_price.html.twig` renders it exactly as it is
							stored and shows a bare number when this deployment has never set
							a currency — see that file for why guessing one would be the
							worse answer.
						#}
						<div class="text-body-secondary small">{{ 'store.price'|trans }}</div>
						<div class="display-6 mb-2">
							{{ include('store/_price.html.twig', {offer: offer, currency: currency, emphasis: ''}) }}
						</div>
						<p class="text-body-secondary mb-0">{{ 'store.price_one_off'|trans }}</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="col-lg-5">
				{#
					**The three sentences, in the order somebody needs them**, and above
					the button rather than beside it. What this does, what it does not do,
					and what happens next.
				#}
				<div class="card h-100">
					<div class="card-body d-flex flex-column">
						<h2 class="h6">{{ 'store.buy_what_happens'|trans }}</h2>
						<ul class="small text-body-secondary">
							<li>{{ 'store.buy_records_request'|trans }}</li>
							<li>{{ 'store.buy_no_charge'|trans }}</li>
							<li>{{ 'store.buy_someone_replies'|trans }}</li>
						</ul>

						<form method="post" class="mt-auto" action="{{ path('store_buy', {module: offer.key}) }}">
							<input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('buy-module') }}">
							{#
								The only input on this page is the CSRF token, and that is the
								property worth stating out loud rather than merely observing:
								there is nothing here for anybody to type. A test asserts it,
								because "we did not add card fields" is the kind of thing a
								later ticket adds by accident while making the page friendlier.
							#}
							<button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary w-100">
								<i class="bi bi-bag" aria-hidden="true"></i>
								{{ 'store.buy_confirm'|trans }}
							</button>
						</form>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</main>
{% endblock %}
